title: Direct I/O prevents database backups from trashing page cache
source: hn
url: https://clickhouse.com/blog/direct-io-managed-postgres-backups
date: '2026-10-17'
authors:
- Kaushik Iska
why_read: Read this to understand how database backups degrade query performance by evicting warm data from the Linux page cache. You will learn how direct I/O combined with stripe-aligned reads mitigates latency spikes on NVMe storage.
hn_id: '50132084'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50132084
---

Running database backups against live primary nodes often degrades query performance in ways that confuse engineers. The issue is usually not raw disk throughput, but rather the operating system page cache. When taking a physical backup, Linux treats large sequential reads like standard file access, populating the cache and ruthlessly evicting the warm pages that your database relies on to serve live queries. Subsequent queries that would have hit memory are suddenly forced into disk reads.

ClickHouse explored this dynamic within their managed PostgreSQL infrastructure. By switching backup streaming reads to Direct I/O with the O_DIRECT flag, the system bypasses the kernel page cache entirely. On their test setup, this approach reduced the latency spike on active queries by roughly two-thirds while lowering CPU consumption by 14 percent.

However, bypassing the page cache also disables kernel readahead. On multi-drive RAID0 NVMe arrays, small unbuffered reads leave disks idling. To overcome this limitation, they had to size each direct read to align with the array stripe and scale concurrent workers explicitly.

Tuning storage engines requires understanding where kernel abstractions stop helping and start hurting your workload.

