Granting autonomous AI agents access to local shells and API tokens creates a massive security surface that traditional process isolation fails to contain. When an agent hallucinates or falls victim to prompt injection, static environment variables and ambient credentials expose your entire infrastructure.

Nono tackles this problem by placing an ephemeral, kernel-enforced boundary around every single tool execution. Instead of passing live credentials like a GitHub token directly to the child process, the supervisor issues a scoped phantom token. The child tool runs in a strictly sandboxed container with bounded standard streams and a read-only workspace.

All outbound network requests are trapped by a local supervisor proxy. This proxy inspects the request target and path, validates whether the action matches the defined argv policy, and swaps the phantom token for the actual secret right at the network boundary. Every execution and authorization outcome is then logged into an immutable SHA-256 Merkle tree.

Runtime security for agents cannot rely on prompt guards alone. Sandboxing tool execution at the operating system level is the only sustainable way to build production agentic workflows.

