Chunked key-value cache compression can drop token retrieval accuracy by up to 40 percentage points depending purely on where the token sits inside a compression window. Average benchmarks completely mask this failure mode.

When serving long-context large language models, inference engines compress consecutive key-value tokens into fewer entries at a fixed stride to save memory bandwidth. Researchers discovered that this fixed boundary introduces an artificial phase coordinate. The exact same token is easy to recall at one offset, but becomes almost invisible to the attention mechanism at another offset.

Mechanistic analysis reveals that different attention heads specialize in distinct phase offsets during training rather than sharing retrieval evenly. Standard benchmarks that measure average recall miss this periodic blindness entirely.

Before deploying aggressive chunked KV compression into production, teams must evaluate retrieval across every phase offset rather than trusting aggregate scores.

