Over sixteen percent of public Model Context Protocol servers expose host filesystem vulnerabilities that allow malicious agents to escape declared tool boundaries. Client-side authorization of a tool call routinely grants unrestricted local process privileges to the host.

A security evaluation of more than 21,000 MCP servers across nine registries identified a vulnerability class termed filesystem capability laundering. Even when an MCP server is categorized as a non-filesystem tool, attacker-steered inputs can traverse path boundaries and exfiltrate sensitive local files such as environment credentials and private keys. The evaluation generated thirteen security advisories, including two CVEs inside the official Anthropic Git MCP server.

The core breakdown happens because modern agent runtimes trust the descriptive tool schema instead of enforcing kernel-level sandboxing around the server process. Path validation checks inside individual tool implementations repeatedly fail against directory traversal and canonicalization bypasses.

Do not rely on prompt guardrails or declared tool schemas to sandbox agent execution; isolate the host process itself.
